# function added() { let total = 0; for (let j = 0; j < 100; j++) total = (total + j * j) | 0; return total; }   as the walk leaves a loop whose head read something that has left the loop:
# the test of the counter stands in the head behind the poll, the head ends in a jump, and the branch stands alone in the block behind.
# So ends the typed build of `for (let i = 0; i < this.values.length; i++) s += this.values[i]`: the read of the length has gone in front, and what is left of its stretch is the jump.
# The turns are laid out all the same: the product stands there once for each of the eight turns laid out and once in the loop as written.
# A head that ends in a jump was taken for a counted loop only where the block behind holds the test: 11.1 instructions a turn for the 7.4 of a list that is a parameter.
